Why, funny that you ask.  Because, I believe that linc.cis.upenn.edu
does have NetHack spoiler files, the best ones, in fact.  The NetHack
source code!

This is the only spoiler file(s) I use.  I have glanced through the
Wheaton College file, and found it to be rather vague, and besides,
reading the source code is much more fun.  The advantages:

1) The source code will give you information that no other spoiler
file will.  For example, the Wheaton file may tell you that something
is possible, but will not give you the probabilities involved.  Every
spoiler file has to miss some stuff, while the source code, by
definition, has it all.

2) Have you ever thought of some feature or change you'd like to make?
Never thought you could actually do it, since you're not much of a
programmer, perhaps, or don't have the time to learn the NetHack
sources?  Well, after enough time using the source code as your
spoiler file, you'll get to know it intimately, and be able to write
in custom features for yourself.  Perhaps add a monster that is a
parody of an administrator at your school or workplace? :-)

